Thought: Let me try a different SMILES format for this compound.
Action: get_formula_from_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "c1ccccc1C2CC2.F[B-](F)(F).[K+]"}

Thought: The formula is C9H10BF3K, which is close but has one extra hydrogen compared to the expected C9H9BF3K. This suggests that the boron is likely directly attached to the cyclopropyl ring, not as a separate ion. Let me try a different structure where the BF3 group is directly attached to the cyclopropyl ring.
Action: get_formula_from_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "c1ccccc1C2C[C@H]2[B-](F)(F)F.[K+]"}

Thought: Great! The formula C9H9BF3K matches the expected formula from the elemental analysis. Let me simulate the spectra to see if it matches the provided NMR data.
Action: simulate_spectra
Action Input: {"smiles": "c1ccccc1C2C[C@H]2[B-](F)(F)F.[K+]"}

Thought: The 13C NMR simulation shows peaks that are somewhat consistent with the experimental data, though not an exact match. Let me try a slightly different structure where the BF3 group is attached to a different position on the cyclopropyl ring.
Action: get_formula_from_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "c1ccccc1[C@@H]1C[C@H]1[B-](F)(F)F.[K+]"}

Thought: This also gives the correct formula. Let me simulate the spectra for this structure.
Action: simulate_spectra
Action Input: {"smiles": "c1ccccc1[C@@H]1C[C@H]1[B-](F)(F)F.[K+]"}

Thought: The 13C NMR simulation is the same as before. Let me try one more structure variation to see if it better matches the experimental data.
Action: get_formula_from_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "c1ccccc1[C@H]1[C@H]([B-](F)(F)F)C1.[K+]"}

Thought: This also gives the correct formula. Let me check if there's any information in PubChem about this type of compound.
Action: relevant_pubchem_sections
Action Input: {"compound": "potassium trifluoro(2-phenylcyclopropyl)borate", "query": "NMR spectra", "top_k": 3}
